    {
      "heading": "Where Mumbai Meets the World",
      "text": "Colaba is where Mumbai has always met the world. The Gateway of India - built in 1924 to commemorate King George V's visit in 1911 - stands at the water's edge as the symbolic entry point to the city, and behind it the Taj Mahal Palace Hotel (1903) has been welcoming the world's most distinguished visitors for over a century. From the Aga Khan to the Beatles, from heads of state to Nobel laureates, the Taj has hosted them all. And the Gateway has watched them all arrive and depart, as it has watched the last British troops leave India in 1948, as it has watched the fishing boats of the Koli community return with their catch every morning before dawn, as it has watched Mumbai grow from a colonial port into one of the world's great cities."
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Colaba Causeway - Mumbai's Most Democratic Space",
      "text": "The Colaba Causeway is one of Mumbai's great democratic institutions. On any given afternoon, you will find a backpacker from Berlin haggling over a leather bag, a Parsi grandmother buying vegetables from a vendor whose family has been selling to hers for three generations, a naval officer's wife browsing antique silverware, a fashion designer looking for vintage fabric and a tourist from Jaipur buying a \"I Love Mumbai\" t-shirt. The Causeway does not discriminate. It serves everyone, at every price point, with the cheerful, relentless energy of a market that has been doing this for over a century. It is, in the truest sense, the soul of Colaba."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Sassoon Dock - The Real Mumbai",
      "text": "If the Gateway of India is Colaba's face to the world, Sassoon Dock is its soul. Built in 1875 as Mumbai's first wet dock, Sassoon Dock is where the Koli fishing community - Mumbai's original inhabitants, who have lived on this peninsula for centuries before the British arrived - brings in its catch every morning before dawn. The pre-dawn fish auction is one of the most extraordinary experiences Mumbai offers: the smell of the sea, the sound of the auction, the lamplight on the wet fish, the Koli women in their distinctive nine-yard saris sorting the catch with practiced efficiency. It is a world that exists just minutes from the Taj Mahal Palace, and most of the hotel's guests never know it is there."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Living in Colaba",
      "text": "To live in Colaba is to live at the intersection of everything Mumbai is and has been. The old families in the heritage buildings of Wodehouse Road carry the memory of a Bombay that no longer exists anywhere else. The naval officers in Navy Nagar bring a discipline and community spirit that is rare in Mumbai's individualistic urban culture. The expats and young professionals who have discovered Colaba's connectivity, culture and character are writing the next chapter of the neighbourhood's story. And the Koli community at Sassoon Dock - fishing the same waters their ancestors fished for centuries - remind everyone that Mumbai was here long before the British, long before the Taj, long before the Gateway of India. Mumbai96 is proud to serve all of them - from the Parsi grandmother who needs a reliable plumber for her Wodehouse Road flat to the young professional who wants the best salon near Mandlik Road."
    }
  ],
